(7409427, 1167327, 14476, '<td valign="top">Great image, Mark.  When I first saw this in voting, I thought it was a copy of a mosaic, and frowned a little.  Then looked at it more closely and realized it was ''real'' !! and also delicious.<br/>As usual with your work, well seen, and well photographed.</td>', 'Great image, Mark.  When I first saw this in voting, I thought it was a copy of a mosaic, and frowned a little.  Then looked at it more closely and realized it was ''real'' !! and also delicious.As usual with your work, well seen, and well photographed.', '2015-10-10 17:07:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(7409435, 1167198, 12611, '<td valign="top">Excellent work and result. To my untrained eye, there is a clarity and "realism" in the mist. And the combination of the two, is something nice to ponder.</td>', 'Excellent work and result. To my untrained eye, there is a clarity and "realism" in the mist. And the combination of the two, is something nice to ponder.', '2015-10-10 18:46:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(7409443, 1167263, 114285, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by tvsometime:</b></div><hr/><i> Beautiful. Looks like a 9am Newark departure. We''ve flown from there numerous occasions and never seen this view. Better timing next time - maybe even better heading to Australia? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks,  actually it was a 7:45 AM flight to Portland Maine, there were still some beautiful golden Hues in the sky from the sun rising.</td>', 'Originally posted by tvsometime: Beautiful. Looks like a 9am Newark departure. We''ve flown from there numerous occasions and never seen this view. Better timing next time - maybe even better heading to Australia?   Thanks,  actually it was a 7:45 AM flight to Portland Maine, there were still some beautiful golden Hues in the sky from the sun rising.', '2015-10-10 22:27:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(7409501, 1167082, 150277, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>Hi Gudjon,<br/> <br/>A solid entry into the low contrast challenge that received an above average score, so well done.  This is a pretty piece of land you chose to photograph, and the clouds made it a perfect fit for the challenge.  <br/> <br/>For me the sun-rays through the clouds (or is that rain?) helped make the image.  And technically your exposure seems right on. The difference between this image and some of the images that were my favorite in this challenge was that I just did not feel strongly pulled into the image.  The location, while pretty, seemed a bit ordinary.  (Although I know that definitely may not be the case, but it just didn''t speak strongly to me that this was a site I would need to see in person).</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club. Hi Gudjon, A solid entry into the low contrast challenge that received an above average score, so well done.  This is a pretty piece of land you chose to photograph, and the clouds made it a perfect fit for the challenge.   For me the sun-rays through the clouds (or is that rain?) helped make the image.  And technically your exposure seems right on. The difference between this image and some of the images that were my favorite in this challenge was that I just did not feel strongly pulled into the image.  The location, while pretty, seemed a bit ordinary.  (Although I know that definitely may not be the case, but it just didn''t speak strongly to me that this was a site I would need to see in person).', '2015-10-11 19:59:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(7409502, 1167370, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Great capture of an animal in its own environment - I could tell from the first glance that this was no zoo shot.  Love the focus on the head, can just imagine how you felt when you met it.  The sinuous body, the duckweed on the surface of the water, the natural lighting, good comp, though maybe a little more dof would be good.  Shooting wide-open is pretty dicey esp with such a shallow dof.<br/> <br/>But DANG!  That branch!  That is definitely a big part of what killed your score imnsho. As already noted, it decapitates the snake and thus robs it of some of its power. That, coupled with the DPC voter fraidy cat voting when it comes to creepy-crawlies and not kutesy kritters or majestic felines/raptors etc....*sigh*<br/> <br/>Still, a very good image.  Maybe just a little more pp contrast would have helped, but cloning out all of that twig would have been a major pain in the butt.<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Great capture of an animal in its own environment - I could tell from the first glance that this was no zoo shot.  Love the focus on the head, can just imagine how you felt when you met it.  The sinuous body, the duckweed on the surface of the water, the natural lighting, good comp, though maybe a little more dof would be good.  Shooting wide-open is pretty dicey esp with such a shallow dof. But DANG!  That branch!  That is definitely a big part of what killed your score imnsho. As already noted, it decapitates the snake and thus robs it of some of its power. That, coupled with the DPC voter fraidy cat voting when it comes to creepy-crawlies and not kutesy kritters or majestic felines/raptors etc....*sigh* Still, a very good image.  Maybe just a little more pp contrast would have helped, but cloning out all of that twig would have been a major pain in the butt. Susan', '2015-10-11 20:02:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(7409503, 1167210, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Very nice setting, great time of day to shoot, and as noted, very dfreamy.  Problem is, there is nothing happening.  There is no true focal point. I looked everywhere for that one thing to rivet my attention and didn''t find it.  Pathway, river, trees, setting sun...that''s it.  It''s like looking at a set before the extras and talent are called on.  Even just a pedestrian or dogwalker on the path would have broken the vista and given us something to look at.  If you had a tripod with you, you could set it up, put on the timer and use yourself as a model.<br/> <br/>Otherwise, I see it''s a kit lens that caught this image. I think you could have slowed the shutter speed down quite a lot (leaning against a tree to stabilize yourself, you should be able to get a fairly sharp image at 1/40) and thus bought yourself some more dof.<br/> <br/>Hope this has been of help, feel free to PM me with any questions.<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Very nice setting, great time of day to shoot, and as noted, very dfreamy.  Problem is, there is nothing happening.  There is no true focal point. I looked everywhere for that one thing to rivet my attention and didn''t find it.  Pathway, river, trees, setting sun...that''s it.  It''s like looking at a set before the extras and talent are called on.  Even just a pedestrian or dogwalker on the path would have broken the vista and given us something to look at.  If you had a tripod with you, you could set it up, put on the timer and use yourself as a model. Otherwise, I see it''s a kit lens that caught this image. I think you could have slowed the shutter speed down quite a lot (leaning against a tree to stabilize yourself, you should be able to get a fairly sharp image at 1/40) and thus bought yourself some more dof. Hope this has been of help, feel free to PM me with any questions. Susan', '2015-10-11 20:10:33.000000', NULL, 0),
